  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Direct experimental evidence**\n\n- **Human bone‑marrow MSCs (in‑vitro):** Icariin (ICA) dose‑dependently increased alkaline‑phosphatase (ALP) activity and the protein levels of bone‑sialoprotein‑II and Runx2, while suppressing adipogenic markers (PPAR‑γ, C/EBP‑α).  The effect required miR‑23a‑mediated activation of the Wnt/β‑catenin pathway and was abolished by DKK1 inhibition【PMID:33743412】 *human‑cell evidence*.\n\n- **Mouse MC3T3‑E1 pre‑osteoblasts (in‑vitro):** ICA (≤100 µM) enhanced ALP activity, mineralized nodule formation, and up‑regulated miR‑153 together with Runx2 protein.  Luciferase assays confirmed miR‑153 directly targets the Runx2 3′‑UTR, and ICA rescued the loss of Runx2 caused by miR‑153 inhibition【PMID:29904399】 *animal‑cell evidence*.\n\n- **Mouse MC3T3‑E1 cells exposed to LPS (in‑vitro):** ICA restored LPS‑induced suppression of osteogenesis, normalising Runx2 protein (not mRNA), decreasing miR‑34c, and inhibiting JNK, p38 and NF‑κB phosphorylation.  Over‑expression of miR‑34c blocked ICA‑stimulated ALP, Runx2, OPN and BMP2 expression【PMID:27492554】 *animal‑cell evidence*.\n\n- **Rat ovariectomy‑induced osteoporosis (in‑vivo):** Oral ICA or BMSC over‑expression of miR‑335‑5p increased bone formation, improved micro‑CT parameters and reduced PTEN expression, indicating that ICA promotes osteogenesis via the miR‑335‑5p/PTEN axis【PMID:35292240】 *animal‑in‑vivo evidence*.\n\n- **Rat titanium‑particle osteolysis model (in‑vivo & in‑vitro):** ICA (10⁻⁸ M pretreatment) rescued Ti‑particle‑induced loss of ALP activity, BMP2, Runx2 and osteocalcin, and up‑regulated miR‑21‑5p.  AntagomiR‑21‑5p abolished ICA’s protective effect, linking miR‑21‑5p to ICA‑driven osteogenic differentiation【PMID:295008"
  },
